ﬂÑˇˇ␀␀∞£	‚™™␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀‰␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀"# Analysis Report of Overloaded Node Capacity Warning\n\n|                     |       |\n|---------------------|-------|\n| Anomaly Date        | 2023-10-15 06:56:45  |\n| Anomaly Description | During the inspection, it was found that from 14:56:49 to 15:02:49 on October 15, 2023, the node ins stdload1 in the database was noted to have a value exceeding 100%, precisely 2.17, indicating an abnormal load on the system. This situation triggered a warning alert indicating a potential problem. However, the status shows that it has been resolved. Please remain vigilant to avoid any future issues.  |\n| Root Cause          | Refined Root Cause Analysis <br><br>Identified by CpuExpert<br><br>The predominant issue was high CPU usage, negatively impacting system performance. Root causes identified include:<br><br>1. **Abnormal Patterns in Multiple Metrics:** Abnormal patterns were detected in `node_procs_running`, `node_procs_blocked`, and `node_entropy_available_bits`.<br>    - **node_procs_running**: An increase in this metric, which measures the number of processes currently active on the CPU, suggests a higher CPU load.<br>    - **node_procs_blocked**: This metric signifies the number of processes currently blocking the CPU, a significant rise in such processes can further strain the CPU.<br>    - **node_entropy_available_bits**: This metric indicates system entropy. An elevation in entropy could strain the CPU, complicating effective computation by system processes.<br><br>2. **Concurrent SQL Queries:** The system was running numerous SQL queries concurrently. Databases, being CPU-intensive applications, put a considerable load on the CPU when multiple queries are executed simultaneously.<br><br>These concurrent factors led to a significant rise in CPU usage.<br><br>Identified by WorkloadExpert<br><br>Issues in server performance were traced back to irregular patterns in several network-related metrics:<br><br>1. **node_sockstat_TCP_tw:** An unexpected trend in this metric, which measures the Time Wait state in TCP connections, hints at irregularities in the number of closed yet not fully terminated connections.<br>2. **node_sockstat_TCP_orphan:** A spike in this metric, indicating the number of orphaned TCP sockets, suggests potential issues with the application using the sockets or irregular network operations.<br>3. **irate(node_netstat_Tcp_PassiveOpens):** Abnormal behavior in this metric indicates that the server is accepting connections at an unusually high rate, signifying potential network congestion or an irregular server workload.<br>4. **node_sockstat_TCP_alloc:** An irregular pattern in this metric, which measures the number of all allocated TCP sockets, could suggest abnormal allocation of TCP sockets, potentially overloading the server's networking capabilities.<br>5. **node_sockstat_TCP_inuse:** Unusual trends in this metric, indicating the number of in-use TCP sockets, suggest that an irregularly high number of sockets are in use, potentially straining the server's network load.<br><br>These abnormal patterns in network-related metrics could result in a high server load, potentially causing system performance anomalies and affecting system operations stability.  |\n| Solutions           | **Refined Solutions Recommended by CpuExpert:**<br><br>1. **Optimize the Database Performance:** <br>    - High CPU usage could be a result of concurrent execution of multiple SQL queries. A viable solution to this would be to regulate the number of SQL queries that run concurrently, effectively reducing the CPU load.<br><br>2. **Add Recommended Indexes:**<br>    - `district2d_w_id, d_id`: Indexing these columns can expedite database operations, leading to increased efficiency and overall performance.<br>  <br>    - `district1d_id,d_w_id`: Much like the previous index, indexing these columns can significantly optimize operations performed on them.<br><br>    - `item1i_id`: The execution of queries involving this 'item1' column may contribute to high CPU usage. Thus, adding an index to this column could fast-track these queries and lighten the CPU load.<br><br>    - `stock1s_i_id,s_w_id`: Indexing these columns in the 'stock1' table could expedite searches and enhance overall performance.<br><br>    - `stock2s_i_id,s_w_id`: If the 'stock2' table is also frequently queried, indexing these columns will optimize performance.<br><br>    - `item2i_id, i_data`: This index can improve query performance on the 'item2' table, thereby reducing CPU load.<br><br>    - Note: Indexing should ideally be carried out during a maintenance window or off-peak hours. This is to prevent the resource-heavy initial build process from interfering with regular workload.<br><br>**Refined Solutions Recommended by WorkloadExpert:**<br><br>1. **Adjusting Network Settings**: To handle workloads more effectively, consider modifying your network settings. This may involve changes to configurations such as socket buffer sizes, TCP window scaling, or maximum transmission units (MTUs).<br><br>2. **Optimizing Network-Related Operations**: Review the network-related operations within your applications for any potential improvements or reductions in unnecessary network traffic.<br><br>3. **Regular Monitoring and Tuning**: Systematic monitoring of system's network performance can help identify anomalies early and allow for timely adjustments in configurations.<br><br>4. **System Improvements**: Based on identified network issues, implementing system improvements, such as hardware upgrades, software efficiency improvements, or network capacity enhancements, could solve the problem.<br><br>    - Note: Ensure that these solutions are situation-specific and not the only possible course of action. Different scenarios may require different strategies.  |\n\n## Diagnosis Process\n<br>1. The diagnosis process of CpuExpert:<br>Thought: Now that I have obtained the start and end time of the anomaly, check whether the CPU usage is abnormal during that time period.<br><details open><summary><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #333333\">using Tools:</span></summary><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; line-height: 36px\"><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: row; align-content: center\"><div style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #333333; width: 160px; flex-shrink: 0\">Command Name:</div><div style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #676c90!important;\">whether_is_abnormal_metric</div></div><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: row; align-content: center\"><div style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #333333; width: 160px; flex-shrink: 0\">Arguments:</div><div style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #676c90!important; white-space: pre-wrap\">\"{\\\"start_time\\\": \\\"2023-10-15 14:56:49\\\", \\\"end_time\\\": \\\"2023-10-15 15:02:49\\\", \\\"metric_name\\\": \\\"cpu_usage\\\"}\"</div></div><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: row; align-content: center\"><div style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #333333; width: 160px; flex-shrink: 0\">Command Status:</div><div style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #676c90!important;\">Success</div></div></div></details>\nThe metric cpu_usage is abnormal <br>  <br> <img style=\"border-radius: 8px;\" src=\"data:image/jpg;base64,\">\nThought: Since the CPU usage is abnormal during the anomaly, I must diagnose the cause of the anomaly using the metrics, queries and knowledge gained from cpu_match_diagnose_knowledge.<br><details open><summary><span style=\"font-size: 14px; font-weight: bold; color: #333333\">using Tools:</span></summary><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: column; line-height: 36px\"><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: row; align-content: center\"><div style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #333333; width: 160px; flex-shrink: 0\">Command Name:</div><div style=\"font-size: 14px; color: #676c90!important;\">cpu_match_diagnose_knowledge</div></div><div style=\"display: flex; flex-direction: row; 